WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.701
-Hey, everybody,
thank you for joining us.

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:03.901
I'm Jess Cagle.

00:00:03.901 --> 00:00:05.267
We are here to talk about
"The Morning Show."

00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:09.067
I love "The Morning Show,"
which is an important show,

00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:11.601
but it's also monumentally
entertaining.

00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:14.734
Anything that has Jennifer
Aniston and Billy Crudup

00:00:14.734 --> 00:00:16.934
doing a duet
from "Sweeney Todd,"

00:00:16.934 --> 00:00:18.834
I am in.

00:00:18.834 --> 00:00:24.067
Also for what it says
about power in the media,

00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:27.667
equality, everything that
we're talking about right now,

00:00:27.667 --> 00:00:32.100
this show is taking on
all of those issues.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.434
So you're gonna see
three short panels.

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:38.534
There are -- They are
about 15 minutes each.

00:00:38.534 --> 00:00:40.033
We're gonna hear
from Reese Witherspoon

00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:43.701
and Jennifer Aniston,
along with their costars

00:00:43.701 --> 00:00:44.868
and the other producers.

00:00:44.868 --> 00:00:46.300
You're all gonna see some --

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.501
You're also gonna see some
clips from "The Morning Show."

00:00:49.501 --> 00:00:52.467
So I'm gonna bring
in our first panel.

00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:54.000
They're here with me now.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.701
They are Jennifer Aniston,
Billy Crudup,

00:00:58.701 --> 00:01:02.567
Gugu Mbatha-Raw,
and Desean K. Terry.

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:05.767
How are you guys?

00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:07.267
-Very well.
-Doing good.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.567
-Congratulations.

00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:10.567
-Jen and Billy, are you guys
gonna sing that song?

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:13.200
Are they gonna sing
the duet right now?

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.767
-Yes. That is --
That's part of the plan.

00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:17.334
-Desean, I think
you're well aware

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:20.000
that that was all a recording,
okay?

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.667
-Bill, you're not gonna tell
them we actually recorded

00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:23.133
the entire show?

00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:26.501
That's what actually this whole
hour is going to be.

00:01:26.501 --> 00:01:29.734
-My entire performance
was dubbed by Glenn Close.

00:01:29.734 --> 00:01:31.300
So, uh...

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.968
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.968 --> 00:01:35.734
-I would be so happy with that
entire show, by the way.

00:01:35.734 --> 00:01:39.501
Jennifer,
I want to start with you...

00:01:39.501 --> 00:01:40.734
-Yes, sir.

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:44.734
-...to start with you
to talk about Alex Levy.

00:01:44.734 --> 00:01:48.667
Some of the things
that fascinated me about Alex

00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:53.434
are she, you know, struggles in
this male-dominated environment.

00:01:53.434 --> 00:01:56.734
She struggles in her life.
She struggles with fame.

00:01:56.734 --> 00:02:00.634
She struggles
with her own complicity in --

00:02:00.634 --> 00:02:04.167
because she feels she helped
create this environment.

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:07.567
What was it -- what was it
about her that intrigued you

00:02:07.567 --> 00:02:11.133
the most when you first started
thinking about this character?

00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:14.133
And as the show evolved
from the premiere

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:16.968
through the finale
and the scripts came in,

00:02:16.968 --> 00:02:21.367
what surprised you
about her evolution?

00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:26.834
-Well, first of all,

00:02:26.834 --> 00:02:32.434
everything that you just listed
is what intrigued me about her.

00:02:32.434 --> 00:02:37.968
And to be a woman
in this day and age

00:02:37.968 --> 00:02:41.968
and understanding
once I really got in

00:02:41.968 --> 00:02:46.400
and understood the world
behind this,

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.534
you know, machine of ma and pa,

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:54.133
and our sort of comfort zone
every morning when we wake up,

00:02:54.133 --> 00:02:56.434
I was -- I was -- I mean,

00:02:56.434 --> 00:03:01.033
what intrigued me about her
was how complicated

00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:03.067
and how she was able
to kind of manage

00:03:03.067 --> 00:03:05.067
all of these spinning plates

00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:10.734
while being completely able
to compartmentalize each plate

00:03:10.734 --> 00:03:15.567
that she spun and when she got
on camera and spoke to America,

00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:18.667
you would not have
a clue of --

00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:21.300
that her life was kind of
crumbling around her.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.968
And that was something
after speaking

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:25.534
to a lot of incredible people
that allowed me

00:03:25.534 --> 00:03:30.100
to talk to them
when we were doing our research.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:31.801
That's what -- you can't --
you can't have --

00:03:31.801 --> 00:03:34.267
There's no world
there's no world in which

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:35.734
any of your life matters.

00:03:35.734 --> 00:03:38.267
It's just really
all about America.

00:03:38.267 --> 00:03:42.501
And that ability
to kind of compartmentalize

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:44.567
is astounding to me.

00:03:44.567 --> 00:03:48.567
And also the drive to keep
that relevance for as long --

00:03:48.567 --> 00:03:53.467
I mean, to really love
that job so much that you

00:03:53.467 --> 00:03:57.567
for 15, 25 years
in some cases or longer,

00:03:57.567 --> 00:03:59.934
will wake up at 3:00
in the morning,

00:03:59.934 --> 00:04:02.334
live this sort of vampire
lifestyle

00:04:02.334 --> 00:04:07.234
and love the news that much
that you can make

00:04:07.234 --> 00:04:09.467
that kind of --
those great sacrifices.

00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:13.400
-The other part was as,
as the scripts came in

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.400
and the show evolved,

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.601
and as your performance
evolved

00:04:17.601 --> 00:04:19.100
and your take on the character,

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.300
was there anything
about Alex

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:24.167
that surprised you by the time
we got to the end?

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:30.167
-I mean, I don't -- I'm not
really sure if anything surpr--

00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:31.501
I just loved --

00:04:31.501 --> 00:04:36.067
I enjoyed every turn
Kerry Ehrin took with Alex.

00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:38.667
I -- Nothing
actually surprised me.

00:04:38.667 --> 00:04:42.434
I think probably -- and I think

00:04:42.434 --> 00:04:44.501
because we also were in constant
communication --

00:04:44.501 --> 00:04:45.767
Reese and myself, Kerry,

00:04:45.767 --> 00:04:48.133
and the writers
about where this show sort of

00:04:48.133 --> 00:04:50.734
was going to evolve
from the beginning to the end.

00:04:50.734 --> 00:04:52.968
So we were -- We kind of
had an idea

00:04:52.968 --> 00:04:58.167
of where we -- where
our characters would be going.

00:04:58.167 --> 00:05:01.834
So there was nothing that
really surprised me so much.

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:05.834
But I think what was really
just surprising,

00:05:05.834 --> 00:05:10.634
honestly, was just how --
how exciting it was to be --

00:05:10.634 --> 00:05:12.133
And this wasn't a surprise.

00:05:12.133 --> 00:05:14.868
I've felt it from the minute
we started doing it,

00:05:14.868 --> 00:05:16.400
was just how exciting it was

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:20.467
to be a part of this project
at this time

00:05:20.467 --> 00:05:25.334
in the world where all of this
was happening around us

00:05:25.334 --> 00:05:29.200
and being able
to be a voice for so many women

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:33.067
and speak and feel that fury,

00:05:33.067 --> 00:05:35.601
especially towards the end
of the series,

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:38.667
where when they just
kind of lose it.

00:05:38.667 --> 00:05:44.100
And just that shame of knowing
that you stood by for --

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:45.868
for so long, just because,

00:05:45.868 --> 00:05:47.667
you know, "Well,
how are we gonna beat that?

00:05:47.667 --> 00:05:49.868
How are we gonna beat
corporate America?

00:05:49.868 --> 00:05:53.534
We're just a bunch of women.
We won't have a voice."

00:05:53.534 --> 00:05:55.434
And yet it turns out,
you actually do.

00:05:55.434 --> 00:05:56.901
And it takes a movement.

00:05:56.901 --> 00:06:02.234
I mean, just being a part of
a movement is quite incredible.

00:06:02.234 --> 00:06:03.667
So...

00:06:03.667 --> 00:06:07.100
-Gugu,
I assume that you knew

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:11.100
a lot about Hannah
and her mind-set

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:14.834
and the trauma that she went
through from the very beginning,

00:06:14.834 --> 00:06:15.968
you knew that.

00:06:15.968 --> 00:06:17.734
The audience,
of course, did not

00:06:17.734 --> 00:06:19.234
in the early episodes.

00:06:19.234 --> 00:06:21.834
What was it like
to build that character,

00:06:21.834 --> 00:06:24.767
knowing what you knew about her?

00:06:24.767 --> 00:06:28.267
But to the audience and to the
other characters,

00:06:28.267 --> 00:06:29.567
she seems okay.

00:06:29.567 --> 00:06:32.067
How did you -- How did you
inhabit that character,

00:06:32.067 --> 00:06:35.100
knowing her trauma,
but giving her the face

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:38.667
that she gives
to the rest of the world?

00:06:38.667 --> 00:06:40.968
-Yeah, well, Kerry Ehrin,
our show runner,

00:06:40.968 --> 00:06:42.434
and Mimi Leder, as you say,

00:06:42.434 --> 00:06:44.968
talked to me
through the whole arc

00:06:44.968 --> 00:06:46.834
of Hannah's journey
from the outset,

00:06:46.834 --> 00:06:48.567
even though I hadn't read
all the scripts.

00:06:48.567 --> 00:06:51.868
And, you know, I always feel
and I think, you know,

00:06:51.868 --> 00:06:55.033
most actors would agree that
the most interesting characters

00:06:55.033 --> 00:06:57.400
to play
are ones that have secrets.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:02.367
And so for me, it really charged
every scene for me,

00:07:02.367 --> 00:07:05.701
even if I didn't have
a lot of dialogue or, you know,

00:07:05.701 --> 00:07:10.300
that I knew I had --
I always had something to feel

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:12.534
and I always had something
to think about the situation.

00:07:12.534 --> 00:07:14.567
And as in life,

00:07:14.567 --> 00:07:18.033
you know, we're always
concealing our trauma.

00:07:18.033 --> 00:07:21.067
We always want to look like
we've got it together.

00:07:21.067 --> 00:07:24.000
And, you know,
I think obviously for Hannah,

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:27.100
she definitely had
the sort of bomb of her career,

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:30.033
which she was very good
at her job,

00:07:30.033 --> 00:07:32.300
very ambitious, very driven.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:35.367
And she was really
using that to --

00:07:35.367 --> 00:07:37.634
as a form of denial, really.

00:07:37.634 --> 00:07:39.934
So, so, yeah,
I found that fascinating.

00:07:39.934 --> 00:07:41.400
And I think, you know,
there are little moments

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.200
that we talked about,
you know, early on in the show,

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:47.000
specifically the scene
where, you know,

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:49.734
Hannah goes and visits
Ashley Brown, for example,

00:07:49.734 --> 00:07:52.734
and tries to sort of lure
her onto the show.

00:07:52.734 --> 00:07:56.033
And there are moments there
in the script that, you know,

00:07:56.033 --> 00:07:59.400
knowing Hannah's
internal motivations

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:01.834
and talking to Mimi
about how to play that

00:08:01.834 --> 00:08:03.300
or how not to play that,

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.300
you know, that she was actually,
you know,

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:09.067
somewhat living vicariously
through Ashley Brown,

00:08:09.067 --> 00:08:12.334
that this meant so much to her
in some ways because,

00:08:12.334 --> 00:08:16.534
you know, through Ashley,
you know, who could expose Mitch

00:08:16.534 --> 00:08:17.834
and talk about Mitch
on the show,

00:08:17.834 --> 00:08:21.100
she was able to kind of feel
that that was,

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.400
you know,
something personal to her.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.334
So there were little
moments like that, you know,

00:08:25.334 --> 00:08:28.701
and when Mitch comes back
into the studio

00:08:28.701 --> 00:08:30.567
to sort of plead with everyone,

00:08:30.567 --> 00:08:32.234
you know, everybody's thought
process obviously

00:08:32.234 --> 00:08:34.567
is very personal and different,
so...

00:08:34.567 --> 00:08:37.234
So, yeah, I think, you know,
and for me, obviously, you know,

00:08:37.234 --> 00:08:42.300
as the series kind of went on,
getting to really, you know,

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:45.100
unleash all of that stuff
with the, you know,

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:48.834
the flashback episode in episode
8 was really special.

00:08:48.834 --> 00:08:52.067
I was kind of chomping
at the bit, you know,

00:08:52.067 --> 00:08:54.000
to get to the meat of her story.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:58.300
So, yeah, it definitely
made it satisfying for me.

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:00.200
-I forgot to say
in the very beginning,

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:02.501
I am gonna go through this
because we're all remote.

00:09:02.501 --> 00:09:03.834
I'm gonna ask, you know,

00:09:03.834 --> 00:09:05.968
I'm gonna direct my questions
to you individually.

00:09:05.968 --> 00:09:08.634
But anytime you have something
to add to somebody's answer

00:09:08.634 --> 00:09:10.567
or whatever,
feel free to jump in

00:09:10.567 --> 00:09:14.234
and feel free
to interrupt me all you want.

00:09:14.234 --> 00:09:18.634
Billy, Cory is this
fascinating character.

00:09:18.634 --> 00:09:20.133
-I do have something to add.

00:09:20.133 --> 00:09:21.834
-Yes. Yes, Jennifer.
-Sorry.

00:09:21.834 --> 00:09:24.701
I just want to say
Gugu in that episode

00:09:24.701 --> 00:09:28.534
was so absolutely phenomenal
and heartbreaking.

00:09:28.534 --> 00:09:31.100
It still gives --
The chills and tears.

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.267
And I've watched it
so many times.

00:09:33.267 --> 00:09:35.033
I mean, all of you have,
obviously.

00:09:35.033 --> 00:09:40.200
But that was so delicate and
really so beautifully handled.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.467
And my God, I mean,
I have chills right now.

00:09:42.467 --> 00:09:45.667
I just wanted to give you
a very large shout-out

00:09:45.667 --> 00:09:49.734
to that
extraordinary performance.

00:09:49.734 --> 00:09:52.434
-That means so much.

00:09:52.434 --> 00:09:54.601
-I've gone back and watched
a few of the episodes,

00:09:54.601 --> 00:09:56.734
and when you know
what has happened

00:09:56.734 --> 00:09:59.300
and to all of them,
to all of you,

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:04.000
and when you go back
and look at that performance

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.734
or an actor's performance,
knowing what you know about them

00:10:06.734 --> 00:10:08.501
and knowing the secrets
that get unveiled later,

00:10:08.501 --> 00:10:12.300
it's really remarkable how
all of you kept all of that in.

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:14.400
It's -- it's a fascinating --

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.567
I totally encourage
a second viewing of the show.

00:10:17.567 --> 00:10:20.267
Billy, when we start --

00:10:20.267 --> 00:10:23.167
I just didn't know
what to make about Cory.

00:10:23.167 --> 00:10:26.267
I think now I maybe got him
figured out a little bit.

00:10:26.267 --> 00:10:27.334
-Why?

00:10:29.434 --> 00:10:32.000
-I think he's --
I think he's a good guy

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:35.367
for the most part,
but in the beginning,

00:10:35.367 --> 00:10:38.167
we just don't --
You just don't have any idea.

00:10:38.167 --> 00:10:40.634
So I'm wondering
if part of the fun

00:10:40.634 --> 00:10:43.167
is he keeps
everybody off balance.

00:10:43.167 --> 00:10:47.067
That's his -- that's his power.
Right? Whoever is with him.

00:10:47.067 --> 00:10:49.667
So how much did you know
about him going in?

00:10:49.667 --> 00:10:52.167
Did you know everything
about him or did you learn stuff

00:10:52.167 --> 00:10:54.634
along the way
like the rest of us did?

00:10:54.634 --> 00:10:57.467
-Well, I probably knew
a little bit more.

00:10:57.467 --> 00:11:00.434
But just to echo Jen's sentiment
real quick,

00:11:00.434 --> 00:11:02.767
the first thing I thought
when I saw Gugu

00:11:02.767 --> 00:11:05.667
and we did some press
after the --

00:11:05.667 --> 00:11:08.767
after the show
came out and everything,

00:11:08.767 --> 00:11:10.133
but the first thing I thought
when I saw her is,

00:11:10.133 --> 00:11:11.701
"Oh, I'm so happy to see you."

00:11:11.701 --> 00:11:16.067
Like -- like she's not dead,
like that character, you know.

00:11:16.067 --> 00:11:18.234
It was so painful.

00:11:18.234 --> 00:11:19.968
And like in my memory,
I was like, "Oh, my God,

00:11:19.968 --> 00:11:22.868
you look so great
for being dead and all that."

00:11:22.868 --> 00:11:23.767
[ Laughter ]

00:11:23.767 --> 00:11:24.767
But...

00:11:24.767 --> 00:11:27.667
-You know, can I say
I'm haunted

00:11:27.667 --> 00:11:31.400
by what the moment
in the -- in the bedroom,

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:34.200
but also the smoke
on the cobblestone

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:36.033
when she disappeared.

00:11:36.033 --> 00:11:38.100
It's so, so heartbreaking.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.667
-It is.
It's really haunting.

00:11:40.667 --> 00:11:41.868
-When you go back and...

00:11:41.868 --> 00:11:43.000
-It's just right.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:44.400
I mean, it should haunt you.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:47.801
It's that kind of thing that not
only haunts a person's life

00:11:47.801 --> 00:11:50.267
but haunts the community
around you.

00:11:50.267 --> 00:11:55.200
And it's the specter
of that kind of intimidation

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:56.734
that creates a culture

00:11:56.734 --> 00:12:00.701
where people don't know
how to behave in a way

00:12:00.701 --> 00:12:02.334
that they were taught
when they were younger.

00:12:02.334 --> 00:12:04.801
Like, how do we --
The golden rule.

00:12:04.801 --> 00:12:06.267
Let's just start there.

00:12:06.267 --> 00:12:08.667
You know, how about things
that are obvious to all of us?

00:12:08.667 --> 00:12:14.167
They become so confused and...

00:12:14.167 --> 00:12:16.501
And they're manipulated
by people

00:12:16.501 --> 00:12:19.067
who like to be
in positions of power.

00:12:19.067 --> 00:12:22.367
Cory is a person who likes
to be in a position of power.

00:12:22.367 --> 00:12:26.033
And one of the ways
that you can manipulate...

00:12:26.033 --> 00:12:28.033
I don't know
if you've watched the news,

00:12:28.033 --> 00:12:31.434
but just keep people
guessing about what's next.

00:12:31.434 --> 00:12:33.767
And you keep people
so discombobulated

00:12:33.767 --> 00:12:35.300
and confused that,

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:39.067
meanwhile, you work underneath
to establish rules

00:12:39.067 --> 00:12:41.501
that you think are going
to ultimately benefit you.

00:12:41.501 --> 00:12:45.567
And I think Cory,

00:12:45.567 --> 00:12:48.467
even though we don't know
what his motives are underneath,

00:12:48.467 --> 00:12:52.334
whether or not there
is any kind of moral ethic,

00:12:52.334 --> 00:12:54.701
he has a very clear
sense of purpose.

00:12:54.701 --> 00:12:56.567
And whether that sense
of purpose

00:12:56.567 --> 00:12:57.801
is judged after the fact

00:12:57.801 --> 00:13:03.133
as being psychopathic
or magnanimous,

00:13:03.133 --> 00:13:06.033
or he knows he has
an opportunity

00:13:06.033 --> 00:13:10.067
as a person who is
in a position of authority

00:13:10.067 --> 00:13:14.667
and someone who the system
has already promoted

00:13:14.667 --> 00:13:17.467
just by virtue
of being a white man.

00:13:17.467 --> 00:13:21.000
He knows very well
that that's a place of power

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:25.767
and he can't stand the dudes
that wield that

00:13:25.767 --> 00:13:31.501
with the kind of thoughtlessness
and lack of empathy.

00:13:31.501 --> 00:13:36.033
And that, to me,
was Kerry's, like, hidden secret

00:13:36.033 --> 00:13:38.834
that she kind of let me into

00:13:38.834 --> 00:13:43.000
with references
to Cory's mom early on,

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:46.667
because I would say to her,
"What is that about?"

00:13:46.667 --> 00:13:50.434
And she said, "He was
very close to his mom.

00:13:50.434 --> 00:13:52.000
And it's really important to him

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:58.067
that he supports women
in the workplace."

00:13:58.067 --> 00:14:02.300
And so for him to be able
and I mean,

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:05.167
one of the bait and switches
is he thinks Jen's character,

00:14:05.167 --> 00:14:07.701
at the beginning, just isn't
interested in the game anymore,

00:14:07.701 --> 00:14:09.167
which pisses him off.

00:14:09.167 --> 00:14:11.801
He's like, "Get in the game.
You're in a position of power."

00:14:11.801 --> 00:14:12.934
When he realizes then

00:14:12.934 --> 00:14:15.534
that she actually
has that motivation,

00:14:15.534 --> 00:14:19.467
but it had been, like,
suppressed by years

00:14:19.467 --> 00:14:23.467
of this, you know,
systemic oppression,

00:14:23.467 --> 00:14:26.234
he's like, "Sweet.
Let's get on the team.

00:14:26.234 --> 00:14:28.267
Let's ride. Let's go.
Let's take this stuff down."

00:14:28.267 --> 00:14:31.601
And so that, to me,
was a winning,

00:14:31.601 --> 00:14:33.701
if not convoluted approach

00:14:33.701 --> 00:14:38.434
to unveiling
a person's moral ethic.

00:14:38.434 --> 00:14:41.367
-It was a good reveal.
Desean...

00:14:41.367 --> 00:14:44.767
-Wait. Can I just say that
I want to see Cory's mom?

00:14:44.767 --> 00:14:45.701
[ Laughter ]

00:14:45.701 --> 00:14:47.100
I want to be there.

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:49.000
-I have a very clear
picture of her.

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.634
[ Laughter ]

00:14:51.634 --> 00:14:54.601
-Anything's possible.

00:14:54.601 --> 00:14:57.400
-Desean, you play Daniel,
the weekend anchor,

00:14:57.400 --> 00:14:59.300
which enabled --

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:02.501
It enables the show to explore

00:15:02.501 --> 00:15:05.434
what this environment is like
for another kind of person,

00:15:05.434 --> 00:15:08.968
a black man
trying to navigate it,

00:15:08.968 --> 00:15:13.667
trying to, you know,
move up in this environment.

00:15:13.667 --> 00:15:15.601
Did -- what do you think

00:15:15.601 --> 00:15:18.634
was going through Daniel's mind
as you thought

00:15:18.634 --> 00:15:21.067
about the character
and inhabited that character?

00:15:21.067 --> 00:15:24.734
He's in that environment.
What is going through his mind?

00:15:26.734 --> 00:15:30.801
-Well, you know, Daniel is --
He's so ambitious,

00:15:30.801 --> 00:15:32.801
and I think that,

00:15:32.801 --> 00:15:35.033
you know, what's fascinating
about the workplace

00:15:35.033 --> 00:15:36.067
is it's this --

00:15:36.067 --> 00:15:37.934
First,
it's a glitzy world, right,

00:15:37.934 --> 00:15:39.334
because you have cameras,

00:15:39.334 --> 00:15:42.100
and then it's such
this capitalist world.

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:45.267
And Daniel makes it very clear
from the very beginning

00:15:45.267 --> 00:15:47.968
that he -- he comes to play.

00:15:47.968 --> 00:15:49.968
And I think what's also
fascinating about it,

00:15:49.968 --> 00:15:52.300
or one of the things that
I really have fun with

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:55.234
with this character
and with this world is,

00:15:55.234 --> 00:15:58.868
that we, because I do count them
as artists,

00:15:58.868 --> 00:16:00.467
journalists,
they're communicators.

00:16:00.467 --> 00:16:05.167
So that means that we're --
we're deeply empaths.

00:16:05.167 --> 00:16:06.868
And so you're connected.

00:16:06.868 --> 00:16:10.634
And so I think it's really
interesting to see the dynamics

00:16:10.634 --> 00:16:14.267
between the things that make
them human and the things that,

00:16:14.267 --> 00:16:16.300
"Oh, this is my ambition
speaking"

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:17.734
or, you know, that moment

00:16:17.734 --> 00:16:20.734
when, you know, I really love
where the series ends

00:16:20.734 --> 00:16:23.200
because, you know, Daniel is in
a point of conflict.

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:28.067
And then we see this brave act
being done by Alex.

00:16:28.067 --> 00:16:29.501
And the question has been,
"Well, what's going on

00:16:29.501 --> 00:16:31.567
through Daniel's head
at that point in time?"

00:16:31.567 --> 00:16:33.834
I think at the base of all
of our characters,

00:16:33.834 --> 00:16:35.167
for the most part,

00:16:35.167 --> 00:16:37.234
we have moments
where we don't trust them.

00:16:37.234 --> 00:16:39.100
But I think one of the things
I love about the show

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:40.467
is that at the base
of the characters,

00:16:40.467 --> 00:16:42.634
you can see that there's
something good about them.

00:16:42.634 --> 00:16:48.000
And I believe at that moment,
Daniel just feels the pain

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:51.834
and empathy of what he,
as a man,

00:16:51.834 --> 00:16:53.601
has also been supporting

00:16:53.601 --> 00:16:56.200
because
he is not guilty out of this.

00:16:56.200 --> 00:16:58.467
All of us have different types
of privilege, right?

00:16:58.467 --> 00:16:59.734
So there's different types
of privilege

00:16:59.734 --> 00:17:01.634
that he has in that moment.

00:17:01.634 --> 00:17:05.167
And in that structure,
he did contribute to that.

00:17:05.167 --> 00:17:07.601
But also, as a black man,
he is the one

00:17:07.601 --> 00:17:10.300
who is experiencing really
and truly

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:15.634
the lens of oppression
based off of color,

00:17:15.634 --> 00:17:18.734
because it is a very,
very colorized world

00:17:18.734 --> 00:17:22.501
where you don't see people
like him at the anchor desk.

00:17:22.501 --> 00:17:26.100
And I think it's really brave
and amazing to have a character

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:28.400
like that who's speaking
out front about it

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:31.300
and so purposely
is trying to change the world.

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:34.701
And just getting his viewership
does change the world.

00:17:34.701 --> 00:17:37.968
Just seeing that image
changes the world.

00:17:37.968 --> 00:17:41.000
And Daniel is very much
so aware of that power

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:43.300
and he wants to wield it.

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:47.667
I think to create that image
that he wasn't able to see

00:17:47.667 --> 00:17:49.100
when he was a young kid

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:53.434
because he knows how powerful
that can be to a viewer.

00:17:53.434 --> 00:17:57.434
-Jennifer and Billy,
whenever you were doing

00:17:57.434 --> 00:18:00.901
the "Sweeney Todd" duet,
where you're sitting there,

00:18:00.901 --> 00:18:03.601
having given your relationship,

00:18:03.601 --> 00:18:06.567
you are really rivals
at that point

00:18:06.567 --> 00:18:09.067
and you are singing
to each other,

00:18:09.067 --> 00:18:11.234
you know, obviously a song
that says

00:18:11.234 --> 00:18:13.934
nothing's gonna harm you,
not while I'm around.

00:18:13.934 --> 00:18:17.067
I was so curious what you
thought those characters

00:18:17.067 --> 00:18:19.234
thought during that song,

00:18:19.234 --> 00:18:22.567
because they're
both smart people.

00:18:22.567 --> 00:18:25.634
They must have seen the irony
in what they were singing.

00:18:25.634 --> 00:18:28.267
But were they also maybe wanting
their relationship

00:18:28.267 --> 00:18:30.367
to be that way, but it's not?

00:18:30.367 --> 00:18:33.934
What do you think was going
through Alex and Cory's head?

00:18:33.934 --> 00:18:37.300
-I still don't know,
by the way.

00:18:37.300 --> 00:18:40.033
But I mean, I think it was --

00:18:40.033 --> 00:18:42.801
I think there -- there was --

00:18:42.801 --> 00:18:46.801
there's an extreme
mutual admiration for each other

00:18:46.801 --> 00:18:48.734
in terms of who they were.

00:18:48.734 --> 00:18:49.968
They're both --

00:18:49.968 --> 00:18:53.834
I think she knows
what Cory is all about.

00:18:53.834 --> 00:18:56.534
And he knows
what Alex is all about.

00:18:56.534 --> 00:18:59.167
But there was something
very bizarrely seductive

00:18:59.167 --> 00:19:01.567
about the entire --

00:19:01.567 --> 00:19:06.501
that -- that interaction
that was sort of,

00:19:06.501 --> 00:19:08.801
you got to take away
whatever it was

00:19:08.801 --> 00:19:11.200
that you wanted to take away.

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:14.367
Your imagination
could just kind of,

00:19:14.367 --> 00:19:16.200
you know, have at it.

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:18.234
But I think it was just
sort of a, you know,

00:19:18.234 --> 00:19:23.868
it was like a sparring of,
you know, with each other.

00:19:23.868 --> 00:19:26.601
It was very fueled.

00:19:26.601 --> 00:19:28.801
But, yes,
it was kind of up to --

00:19:28.801 --> 00:19:30.901
I'm still very --
We're still very baffled.

00:19:30.901 --> 00:19:32.234
I am still somewhat baffled.

00:19:32.234 --> 00:19:34.701
-For us watching it
in the room and we saw it...

00:19:34.701 --> 00:19:36.267
-Yeah, what did you guys think?

00:19:36.267 --> 00:19:38.467
-...we were like, "Uh,
they about to bone."

00:19:38.467 --> 00:19:41.701
[ Laughing ]

00:19:41.701 --> 00:19:45.067
-It was very confusing.

00:19:45.067 --> 00:19:47.834
-It was rich.
I want to hand it to all of you.

00:19:47.834 --> 00:19:49.200
I know that, you know,

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:51.133
you were all talking
about Gugu's performance.

00:19:51.133 --> 00:19:54.334
And I noticed in the --
in the finale, you know,

00:19:54.334 --> 00:19:55.567
you hear her say --

00:19:55.567 --> 00:19:57.033
Really a throwaway line.

00:19:57.033 --> 00:19:58.267
You didn't make a lot of this,

00:19:58.267 --> 00:20:00.767
where she says,
"It didn't kill me."

00:20:00.767 --> 00:20:02.868
And knowing what --
if you --

00:20:02.868 --> 00:20:05.767
if it's a second viewing
and you know what's happening,

00:20:05.767 --> 00:20:08.267
that is a really
shattering line.

00:20:08.267 --> 00:20:10.601
And I have to hand it
to all of you.

00:20:10.601 --> 00:20:12.801
Great show.
Great performances.

00:20:12.801 --> 00:20:14.033
Thank you for it.

00:20:14.033 --> 00:20:16.033
I've now watched it twice.

00:20:16.033 --> 00:20:17.667
I'll watch it again.

00:20:17.667 --> 00:20:20.434
Anyway we are --
we're out of time

00:20:20.434 --> 00:20:22.501
and we're gonna have a clip

00:20:22.501 --> 00:20:26.667
and then we're gonna be back
with our next panel.

00:20:26.667 --> 00:20:28.200
-I didn't know --

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:29.734
-What about the boys' club that
you told me would never change.

00:20:29.734 --> 00:20:30.634
What about that?

00:20:33.067 --> 00:20:35.701
-I...I don't know.

00:20:37.701 --> 00:20:38.601
I did...I said it.

00:20:38.601 --> 00:20:41.367
I didn't realize
how...f--- up.

00:20:41.367 --> 00:20:44.100
I just [sighs]

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:45.367
-Goodbye, Alex.

00:20:45.367 --> 00:20:47.501
-No, Bradley, I cannot do this.

00:20:47.501 --> 00:20:49.334
I need you to get me through
today. I just need you --

00:20:49.334 --> 00:20:50.634
-I can't do it!

00:20:50.634 --> 00:20:51.934
-I just need you for today.

00:20:51.934 --> 00:20:53.067
-"The Morning Show." Can I take
a selfie with you?

00:20:53.067 --> 00:20:54.300
-It's really not a good time.
I'm sorry.

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:55.467
Hey, f--- off!
-Ow!

00:20:55.467 --> 00:20:57.300
-I said no!
Give me your f--- camera.

00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:59.334
-Alex. Alex!
-You f---!

00:20:59.334 --> 00:21:00.501
What, do you think
you own me?

00:21:00.501 --> 00:21:02.367
You think I'm available
to you 24/7

00:21:02.367 --> 00:21:04.067
because I'm in your living room
every morning

00:21:04.067 --> 00:21:05.434
trying to make you feel better
about this miserable world?

00:21:05.434 --> 00:21:06.734
-What are you doing?
-Well, I'm not. Guess what?

00:21:06.734 --> 00:21:07.901
-Alex.
-I'm empty.

00:21:07.901 --> 00:21:09.601
This is empty. It's empty.
-Stop it, Alex.

00:21:09.601 --> 00:21:11.567
-You know what empty looks like?
It looks like this.

00:21:11.567 --> 00:21:13.701
When I say no, it means no.
-Alex! Alex!

00:21:13.701 --> 00:21:15.234
Will you just calm
the f--- down?

00:21:15.234 --> 00:21:17.300
I will go upstairs
if you just shut the f--- up.

00:21:17.300 --> 00:21:18.734
Just give him his phone back.
-Yes, I will.

00:21:18.734 --> 00:21:21.100
-Give him his phone back!
-Here's your f--- phone, sir.

00:21:21.100 --> 00:21:22.868
-Come on. Come on.
-Piece of s---!

00:21:22.868 --> 00:21:26.133
-Leave him. Jesus Christ.
Just today. I'm doing this...

00:21:26.133 --> 00:21:27.901
-I know just today.
I know.

00:21:27.901 --> 00:21:29.400
-Welcome back.

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:33.634
Now on this panel,
we have Reese Witherspoon.

00:21:33.634 --> 00:21:35.701
We have Kerry Ehrin.

00:21:35.701 --> 00:21:39.167
We have Karen Pittman
and Mark Duplass.

00:21:39.167 --> 00:21:42.434
Reese, I'm gonna start with you.

00:21:42.434 --> 00:21:46.234
And in the finale,

00:21:46.234 --> 00:21:48.834
I really wanted to ask you
about the scenes

00:21:48.834 --> 00:21:52.200
that you have
with Gugu Mbatha-Raw,

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:57.434
where Bradley is learning
the extent of her --

00:21:57.434 --> 00:21:59.300
of her trauma.

00:21:59.300 --> 00:22:03.834
But she's also trying to do
her job because Bradley,

00:22:03.834 --> 00:22:05.133
among many other things,

00:22:05.133 --> 00:22:09.133
is a true journalist
in the best sense of the word.

00:22:09.133 --> 00:22:13.033
And I wanted to ask you
how you approached those scenes

00:22:13.033 --> 00:22:14.834
and how you walked
that tightrope

00:22:14.834 --> 00:22:16.734
between having compassion

00:22:16.734 --> 00:22:20.167
and getting her to do
what you need her to do.

00:22:20.167 --> 00:22:23.200
-Oh, yeah. I actually --
That's a great question

00:22:23.200 --> 00:22:25.400
because I think we've
all been thinking a lot

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:30.901
about having trauma-informed
conversations.

00:22:30.901 --> 00:22:32.334
Right?

00:22:32.334 --> 00:22:35.534
I think we're walking
through a space right now

00:22:35.534 --> 00:22:37.300
where basically
everyone we're encountering

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:39.267
is dealing with a lot of trauma.

00:22:39.267 --> 00:22:42.934
And Kerry...

00:22:42.934 --> 00:22:46.200
It's weird to talk about Kerry,
and she's sitting right here.

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:47.601
-Go ahead.

00:22:47.601 --> 00:22:51.801
-You know, I mean, she just
writes brilliantly to the nuance

00:22:51.801 --> 00:22:53.901
of those kind of conversations,
right?

00:22:53.901 --> 00:22:56.968
So and -- and there's different
tracks going on

00:22:56.968 --> 00:22:58.167
at the same time.

00:22:58.167 --> 00:22:59.334
My character's a journalist.

00:22:59.334 --> 00:23:00.734
She's trying to get to the truth

00:23:00.734 --> 00:23:03.567
of this sort
of corporate disease

00:23:03.567 --> 00:23:05.968
that exists
within our network.

00:23:05.968 --> 00:23:10.200
But she's also thoughtful
about Gugu --

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:13.000
her character's experience.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:15.601
But is she -- is she really
being thoughtful

00:23:15.601 --> 00:23:16.801
about Gugu's trauma

00:23:16.801 --> 00:23:21.534
or is she sort of serving
her own ideas

00:23:21.534 --> 00:23:23.767
and trying to fulfill
her own needs?

00:23:23.767 --> 00:23:27.267
So I think we'll see
as the show ends.

00:23:27.267 --> 00:23:32.100
And then as season two begins,
what does it mean

00:23:32.100 --> 00:23:35.767
to charge forward with your own
needs being more important

00:23:35.767 --> 00:23:39.801
than understanding
someone else's drama?

00:23:39.801 --> 00:23:42.901
-Kerry, because
we have you here

00:23:42.901 --> 00:23:45.534
and I want to go
straight to the source,

00:23:45.534 --> 00:23:48.501
I just want to ask you directly,

00:23:48.501 --> 00:23:53.567
what does this show say
about power

00:23:53.567 --> 00:23:58.133
and what does it say about
how we as a culture reckon

00:23:58.133 --> 00:24:01.601
with inequality in our culture?

00:24:03.667 --> 00:24:07.334
-Oh, such a --
I need, like, an hour

00:24:07.334 --> 00:24:10.367
and an essay pencil
to answer that.

00:24:10.367 --> 00:24:12.067
-That's a big question.

00:24:12.067 --> 00:24:14.434
-Break it down
a little bit for me.

00:24:14.434 --> 00:24:16.634
Give me the first part.

00:24:16.634 --> 00:24:19.467
-What did you want to say
with the show?

00:24:19.467 --> 00:24:20.968
The show's about power.

00:24:20.968 --> 00:24:22.701
It's certainly about Me Too.

00:24:22.701 --> 00:24:24.767
But it really is,
at the heart of it,

00:24:24.767 --> 00:24:26.367
it's about power.

00:24:26.367 --> 00:24:28.868
And I'm sure that we'll see that
play out in the second season.

00:24:28.868 --> 00:24:31.200
But what did you want to say

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:34.000
with this show about power
or the abuse of power?

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:35.434
-Power.

00:24:35.434 --> 00:24:38.067
Um, you know, I guess...

00:24:38.067 --> 00:24:42.367
My feeling about power
is that it's --

00:24:42.367 --> 00:24:45.701
it's the strange thing
that attracts all of us

00:24:45.701 --> 00:24:48.400
and usually leads us
off a cliff,

00:24:48.400 --> 00:24:52.434
that it isn't necessarily
feeding any good part of you.

00:24:52.434 --> 00:24:55.234
But it's linked to some sort
of survival thing

00:24:55.234 --> 00:25:00.167
that we left, you know,
thousands of years ago.

00:25:00.167 --> 00:25:02.300
But the power in itself
just has this, like,

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:05.067
magical seduction to it.

00:25:05.067 --> 00:25:07.300
And we just get focused on it

00:25:07.300 --> 00:25:09.968
at the expense
of everything else.

00:25:09.968 --> 00:25:14.234
And just and just
and just drive toward it

00:25:14.234 --> 00:25:15.234
at all costs.

00:25:15.234 --> 00:25:17.801
And I think,
you know, in that sense,

00:25:17.801 --> 00:25:20.767
it's a --
it's a morality play.

00:25:20.767 --> 00:25:23.701
You know, it's "Don't let this
happen to you."

00:25:25.534 --> 00:25:30.968
-When you were, you know,
just starting the first season,

00:25:30.968 --> 00:25:35.300
the conversation
about Me Too erupted.

00:25:35.300 --> 00:25:39.033
Many people were suddenly
very publicly held accountable

00:25:39.033 --> 00:25:42.534
for misconduct and worse.

00:25:42.534 --> 00:25:45.801
And right now, as you're going
into the second season,

00:25:45.801 --> 00:25:50.067
another cultural conversation
is happening,

00:25:50.067 --> 00:25:53.000
long overdue, about race.

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:55.501
How is that informing
or changing

00:25:55.501 --> 00:25:57.834
what you're going
to do in the second season?

00:25:57.834 --> 00:26:01.767
-Well, we're working
on the second season

00:26:01.767 --> 00:26:04.901
because we have the time
basically to kind of go

00:26:04.901 --> 00:26:07.734
and dig in
and reinvent it a little bit.

00:26:07.734 --> 00:26:11.934
And we're figuring --
we're figuring all that out.

00:26:11.934 --> 00:26:15.200
I said recently that you...

00:26:15.200 --> 00:26:18.767
like, creative people
are just --

00:26:18.767 --> 00:26:23.667
We just absorb everything
and then we repurpose it.

00:26:23.667 --> 00:26:26.601
So that's what we're
in the process of doing.

00:26:26.601 --> 00:26:30.734
I mean, it has been a huge...

00:26:30.734 --> 00:26:35.767
huge eye-opening
and emotional time

00:26:35.767 --> 00:26:39.534
that I think is going
to be present

00:26:39.534 --> 00:26:44.434
in so many artists' work.

00:26:44.434 --> 00:26:46.734
-One of the things
that is so great

00:26:46.734 --> 00:26:48.200
and compelling
and interesting about the show

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:52.200
is that it takes
on all of these issues of power,

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:56.934
but it not only doesn't shy
away from the gray areas,

00:26:56.934 --> 00:26:58.367
it really runs toward them.

00:26:58.367 --> 00:27:05.033
And Karen, you and Mitch,
your relationship,

00:27:05.033 --> 00:27:07.734
at least from what I can
interpret from the show

00:27:07.734 --> 00:27:11.634
was a very gray area.

00:27:11.634 --> 00:27:14.434
And when you learned
that she had had --

00:27:14.434 --> 00:27:17.100
that your character
had a "consensual"

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:21.767
relationship with Mitch,

00:27:21.767 --> 00:27:23.601
how did you respond to that?

00:27:23.601 --> 00:27:27.267
Did you think, "Oh, my gosh,
this is a terrible message"

00:27:27.267 --> 00:27:30.334
or, "Oh, my gosh,
this is a really interesting

00:27:30.334 --> 00:27:33.300
kind of relationship
to look at"?

00:27:33.300 --> 00:27:35.467
-No, it's absolutely
interesting.

00:27:35.467 --> 00:27:36.834
It's very challenging.

00:27:36.834 --> 00:27:38.133
I love a challenge, though.

00:27:38.133 --> 00:27:40.434
And I thought, again,
the writing staff

00:27:40.434 --> 00:27:43.434
and Kerry Ehrin, I thought
they handled it beautifully

00:27:43.434 --> 00:27:44.667
in a nuanced way.

00:27:44.667 --> 00:27:49.367
I mean, I think that no woman
is not familiar

00:27:49.367 --> 00:27:51.567
with a very charismatic
predator, right?

00:27:51.567 --> 00:27:53.934
That is essentially
what Mitch Kessler is,

00:27:53.934 --> 00:27:56.868
the most charismatic man
you would meet.

00:27:56.868 --> 00:28:02.133
And the...synergy of when
he starts to prey on you,

00:28:02.133 --> 00:28:03.367
you just really --

00:28:03.367 --> 00:28:05.567
You really don't know,
you know?

00:28:05.567 --> 00:28:09.434
I often would reach out to Kerry
every couple weeks,

00:28:09.434 --> 00:28:12.300
would call her,
like, on the weekend

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:13.467
or some moment where I know

00:28:13.467 --> 00:28:15.200
she was probably busy
doing something else

00:28:15.200 --> 00:28:18.634
or text her and say, "Hey,
Kerry, does Mia find her way

00:28:18.634 --> 00:28:19.968
out of it, this episode?"

00:28:19.968 --> 00:28:21.334
[ Laughter ]

00:28:21.334 --> 00:28:23.968
But, no, she doesn't.

00:28:23.968 --> 00:28:27.901
I mean, I think
all the female characters,

00:28:27.901 --> 00:28:29.701
we were all under the gun,

00:28:29.701 --> 00:28:32.100
we were all under
this emotional,

00:28:32.100 --> 00:28:35.300
incredible emotional weight,
this gray area,

00:28:35.300 --> 00:28:37.634
which I thought
was just this, of course,

00:28:37.634 --> 00:28:41.234
that it sort of exploded in
in episode seven for Mia.

00:28:41.234 --> 00:28:43.667
But there's so many
different variations

00:28:43.667 --> 00:28:45.501
of how this worked
for each character

00:28:45.501 --> 00:28:48.234
that had been affected
by Mitch Kessler.

00:28:48.234 --> 00:28:50.534
You had Ashley Brown
and Hannah Schoenfeld

00:28:50.534 --> 00:28:52.467
and, of course, Alex Levy.

00:28:52.467 --> 00:28:56.467
So, yeah, but for me, it was
a particularly gray area.

00:28:56.467 --> 00:28:59.033
But I didn't find that
it was handled any --

00:28:59.033 --> 00:29:01.267
subjectively
or judgmentally at all.

00:29:01.267 --> 00:29:04.033
It wasn't like we were wagging
a finger at her.

00:29:04.033 --> 00:29:07.801
We really were thoughtful
about what her character arc

00:29:07.801 --> 00:29:10.300
looked like throughout
the season.

00:29:10.300 --> 00:29:11.601
-For me, one of the most --

00:29:11.601 --> 00:29:13.334
one of the most
shattering scenes

00:29:13.334 --> 00:29:16.000
is when Mia and Alex

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:19.534
are sitting there and pointing
fingers at each other

00:29:19.534 --> 00:29:21.667
because you realize the toll

00:29:21.667 --> 00:29:25.167
that this kind
of environment takes on people

00:29:25.167 --> 00:29:27.968
when these, you know,
two people that should be allies

00:29:27.968 --> 00:29:31.067
are suddenly sort of pitted
against each other.

00:29:31.067 --> 00:29:33.167
It's really powerful.

00:29:33.167 --> 00:29:35.868
Mark, you play Chip, obviously,

00:29:35.868 --> 00:29:40.100
who is a, you know,
he's a straight, white male.

00:29:40.100 --> 00:29:42.267
Very much part of the system.

00:29:42.267 --> 00:29:44.400
He is -- he's a company man,

00:29:44.400 --> 00:29:48.000
but he becomes
an ally by the end.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:52.033
What can you tell me
about his evolution,

00:29:52.033 --> 00:29:54.267
Chip's evolution,
and what he learns

00:29:54.267 --> 00:29:55.801
and what he has --

00:29:55.801 --> 00:29:58.801
what that character
has to say to men,

00:29:58.801 --> 00:30:01.901
particularly straight, white men
in the workplace?

00:30:01.901 --> 00:30:03.167
-That's a great question.

00:30:03.167 --> 00:30:06.334
You know, it's become
a more complex answer

00:30:06.334 --> 00:30:08.734
since we've made the show.

00:30:08.734 --> 00:30:12.234
And I don't know
that I have a full handle on it,

00:30:12.234 --> 00:30:14.367
but I know that at the time
when we were making the show,

00:30:14.367 --> 00:30:17.367
one thing that really appealed
to me about Chip --

00:30:17.367 --> 00:30:18.567
And you're right.

00:30:18.567 --> 00:30:21.434
Being a straight, white male cog
in that machine

00:30:21.434 --> 00:30:24.701
is that
I think Chip fancied himself

00:30:24.701 --> 00:30:28.701
at least 10% downstream
towards the good side

00:30:28.701 --> 00:30:31.000
of how men in that position
of power could be.

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:33.334
He was a pretty good boss, too,
to the women.

00:30:33.334 --> 00:30:34.801
He was pretty good to people.

00:30:34.801 --> 00:30:38.334
And that was enough
for him in that moment.

00:30:38.334 --> 00:30:40.334
And what I think
the show did so well

00:30:40.334 --> 00:30:43.133
is that as the Me Too movement

00:30:43.133 --> 00:30:46.167
came barreling forward
at an exponential speed,

00:30:46.167 --> 00:30:48.234
he found out
that his 10% downstream

00:30:48.234 --> 00:30:53.267
was woefully behind
for human standards.

00:30:53.267 --> 00:30:55.234
And I think because
he is a decent person,

00:30:55.234 --> 00:30:57.734
it threw him
into emotional turmoil,

00:30:57.734 --> 00:30:59.601
into existential turmoil.

00:30:59.601 --> 00:31:02.334
And I think that he had a lot of
catching up to do

00:31:02.334 --> 00:31:06.434
in order to become an ally
and to -- and to, I guess,

00:31:06.434 --> 00:31:09.934
reckon with his complicitness
in that part of the problem,

00:31:09.934 --> 00:31:11.868
even though
he was a "better version"

00:31:11.868 --> 00:31:15.334
of one of those white,
powerful guys.

00:31:15.334 --> 00:31:19.067
And I have to say, I identify
with that more than ever now

00:31:19.067 --> 00:31:21.767
in 2020
as I think about my own place

00:31:21.767 --> 00:31:23.734
and as a person
who runs a company

00:31:23.734 --> 00:31:25.634
and what story
do we want to tell now?

00:31:25.634 --> 00:31:29.167
And I've always considered
myself to be mostly

00:31:29.167 --> 00:31:32.167
on the right side of things
and now can really see Chip

00:31:32.167 --> 00:31:35.634
and feel him
and say, "I was close,

00:31:35.634 --> 00:31:39.434
but we're all f--- behind."

00:31:39.434 --> 00:31:41.200
And I think that one thing

00:31:41.200 --> 00:31:43.701
this show was oddly
prescient about

00:31:43.701 --> 00:31:47.200
was when the wave comes
and it can't be stopped.

00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:49.334
It comes at a speed that none
of us are prepared for.

00:31:49.334 --> 00:31:50.801
That's not a bad thing.

00:31:50.801 --> 00:31:53.334
You just gotta be willing
to be honest and shift.

00:31:53.334 --> 00:31:55.467
And I have no idea, Kerry,

00:31:55.467 --> 00:31:57.901
how you're gonna handle
that for season two.

00:31:57.901 --> 00:31:59.767
I'm excited to see
what you --

00:31:59.767 --> 00:32:01.667
-Good luck.
-Yeah, good luck.

00:32:01.667 --> 00:32:06.000
-The conversation about racial
and gender diversity in casts

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:08.701
and on crews is relatively new.

00:32:08.701 --> 00:32:10.801
I mean,
it's been had in earnest.

00:32:10.801 --> 00:32:12.767
It is being had more
in earnest now.

00:32:12.767 --> 00:32:14.601
But over the past
couple of years,

00:32:14.601 --> 00:32:17.067
it's been had in earnest.

00:32:17.067 --> 00:32:19.601
Do you find it easier now?

00:32:19.601 --> 00:32:24.334
Has it gotten any easier to find
the people that you want to hire

00:32:24.334 --> 00:32:28.000
and to make sure that the people
with the money allow you

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:29.767
to hire the people
that you want to?

00:32:31.901 --> 00:32:34.167
-That's a great question.

00:32:34.167 --> 00:32:35.467
I have been,

00:32:35.467 --> 00:32:39.868
I mean, working in the Hollywood
world for 30 years

00:32:39.868 --> 00:32:43.734
and I've seen enormous shifts
in the past three years,

00:32:43.734 --> 00:32:48.400
and I have to say,
like, I found a lot of strength

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:50.901
through the Time's Up movement

00:32:50.901 --> 00:32:53.968
by other women
who are super aligned

00:32:53.968 --> 00:32:58.067
with trying to find equity
in our workplace.

00:32:58.067 --> 00:33:00.334
And that's really
intersectional work

00:33:00.334 --> 00:33:03.667
that I had never experienced
before in our business

00:33:03.667 --> 00:33:06.167
and really understanding

00:33:06.167 --> 00:33:07.634
that all of those things
were possible.

00:33:07.634 --> 00:33:11.334
Part of what really
inspired me, too,

00:33:11.334 --> 00:33:15.400
was working with Ava DuVernay
on "Wrinkle in Time."

00:33:15.400 --> 00:33:18.601
And the work that she had done
behind the camera

00:33:18.601 --> 00:33:20.567
to make sure
that the crews were diverse

00:33:20.567 --> 00:33:24.667
and that we were, you know,
how -- what story you tell

00:33:24.667 --> 00:33:27.834
is just as important
as how you tell the story.

00:33:27.834 --> 00:33:30.667
--What are -- what are each
of you and I --

00:33:30.667 --> 00:33:32.434
Kerry, this will be harder
for you to answer.

00:33:32.434 --> 00:33:37.000
But each of you tell me
what relationship or aspect

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:40.734
of your character
you're most excited

00:33:40.734 --> 00:33:43.601
to explore more in season two.

00:33:43.601 --> 00:33:46.167
I'll start with Karen.

00:33:46.167 --> 00:33:47.901
-Yikes.

00:33:47.901 --> 00:33:50.801
Starting with me, are you?

00:33:50.801 --> 00:33:54.267
Um, I think the aspect

00:33:54.267 --> 00:33:57.467
that I'm most interested
in exploring is,

00:33:57.467 --> 00:34:00.601
you know,
Mia walked through season one

00:34:00.601 --> 00:34:02.567
with a great deal of --

00:34:02.567 --> 00:34:05.767
Always walking into a scene
with Mia,

00:34:05.767 --> 00:34:09.467
I had to carry the motivation

00:34:09.467 --> 00:34:12.200
of, "I am gonna turn
this conversation around

00:34:12.200 --> 00:34:15.968
about what happened with Mitch,"
in every moment,

00:34:15.968 --> 00:34:18.634
whether it was with Alex
or with Bradley

00:34:18.634 --> 00:34:21.801
or with Cory or with Chip.

00:34:21.801 --> 00:34:23.868
It was always in
some kind of response

00:34:23.868 --> 00:34:28.300
to, "I need to fix this thing
that I did."

00:34:28.300 --> 00:34:31.133
It felt like every response,
everything

00:34:31.133 --> 00:34:33.434
that she was challenged with
was in some --

00:34:33.434 --> 00:34:36.300
Somewhere in her behavior was
a response to this mistake

00:34:36.300 --> 00:34:39.734
that she made,
this self-inflicted wound.

00:34:39.734 --> 00:34:45.167
So because she addressed it
in episode seven so well

00:34:45.167 --> 00:34:47.267
and we took her
all the way through to

00:34:47.267 --> 00:34:49.801
episode 10, I'm interested
what it will be like

00:34:49.801 --> 00:34:52.534
when Mia is not actually --
every single thing

00:34:52.534 --> 00:34:54.501
that she did in the back
of her mind

00:34:54.501 --> 00:34:58.033
wasn't that f--- that she did
with Mitch Kessler.

00:34:58.033 --> 00:35:00.400
You know, that's gonna be
interesting for me

00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:04.734
to see what her emotional
landscape is gonna look like

00:35:04.734 --> 00:35:05.901
for season two.

00:35:05.901 --> 00:35:07.300
No pressure, Kerry Ehrin.

00:35:07.300 --> 00:35:09.167
-No, yeah, I...

00:35:09.167 --> 00:35:10.701
You won't be disappointed.

00:35:10.701 --> 00:35:13.767
[ Laughter ]

00:35:13.767 --> 00:35:16.334
-Mark, I'm really confused
about Chip.

00:35:16.334 --> 00:35:18.000
Like, is he gonna be back?

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:19.200
What's gonna --

00:35:19.200 --> 00:35:21.934
What's gonna be
happening with Chip?

00:35:21.934 --> 00:35:24.100
Or what are you excited
to know about?

00:35:24.100 --> 00:35:26.501
-Listen, I know some things.

00:35:26.501 --> 00:35:29.801
I don't want to say anything
because I don't want to ruin it.

00:35:29.801 --> 00:35:31.934
And who knows what the hell
Kerry's rewritten at this point.

00:35:31.934 --> 00:35:36.601
But I will say that
what I love about Chip

00:35:36.601 --> 00:35:38.400
is that he is very --
he is very nimble.

00:35:38.400 --> 00:35:39.667
He's good at being on his feet.

00:35:39.667 --> 00:35:41.200
He's good at convincing
people of things.

00:35:41.200 --> 00:35:42.601
But he's a lonely guy.

00:35:42.601 --> 00:35:44.634
And when it comes to his
interpersonal relationships,

00:35:44.634 --> 00:35:46.767
there's a massive black
hole there,

00:35:46.767 --> 00:35:48.834
and I'm looking forward
to seeing

00:35:48.834 --> 00:35:50.634
that little Achilles heel of his

00:35:50.634 --> 00:35:52.367
as it affects
not only his work life,

00:35:52.367 --> 00:35:55.300
but, you know, that real
vulnerability of someone

00:35:55.300 --> 00:35:57.434
once they allow themselves
to get close to someone

00:35:57.434 --> 00:35:59.767
and how
that really is wonderful,

00:35:59.767 --> 00:36:02.467
but also sends a wrecking ball
into your life sometimes.

00:36:02.467 --> 00:36:05.167
-Reese, what about you?
What about Bradley?

00:36:05.167 --> 00:36:06.868
-[ Sighs ]

00:36:06.868 --> 00:36:09.767
I just love Bradley so much.

00:36:09.767 --> 00:36:11.934
[ Laughter ]

00:36:11.934 --> 00:36:15.734
She has this hair-trigger temper
that is just so fun to play.

00:36:15.734 --> 00:36:19.100
And this truth-teller
sensibility

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:21.033
that she just wishes
she could stop

00:36:21.033 --> 00:36:24.033
and she just can't,
and I relate to that so much.

00:36:24.033 --> 00:36:26.567
'Cause I have a big, fat mouth
sometimes.

00:36:26.567 --> 00:36:29.834
[ Laughter ]

00:36:29.834 --> 00:36:34.234
-Glad to know
that that won't stop.

00:36:34.234 --> 00:36:35.467
-No.

00:36:35.467 --> 00:36:38.868
-No, and I love what Kerry
explores with power

00:36:38.868 --> 00:36:40.801
and how that affects people.

00:36:40.801 --> 00:36:42.868
What is power, right?

00:36:42.868 --> 00:36:44.167
And what is ego?

00:36:44.167 --> 00:36:48.067
And what do you do with power,
right, once you have it?

00:36:48.067 --> 00:36:49.400
I'm really excited to see that

00:36:49.400 --> 00:36:52.934
because Bradley is maybe
gonna explore

00:36:52.934 --> 00:36:55.734
having a little more power
in her life.

00:36:55.734 --> 00:36:57.234
-Thank you guys so much.

00:36:57.234 --> 00:37:00.968
Karen Pittman, Mark Duplass,
Kerry Ehrin, Reese Witherspoon.

00:37:00.968 --> 00:37:03.868
We are gonna be back
with our next panel

00:37:03.868 --> 00:37:07.467
right after this clip
from "The Morning Show."

00:37:07.467 --> 00:37:10.000
-I chose you.

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:13.200
And everything is
on the line for me.

00:37:13.200 --> 00:37:16.467
-We have an opportunity to do
something truly meaningful.

00:37:18.000 --> 00:37:21.167
-We're "The Morning Show."
We can do anything.

00:37:21.167 --> 00:37:22.434
-It's my whole life.

00:37:22.434 --> 00:37:25.634
You can't ask me
to not fight for that.

00:37:25.634 --> 00:37:28.300
-We're in the middle
of an epic rebirth.

00:37:28.300 --> 00:37:33.467
♪♪

00:37:33.467 --> 00:37:35.033
-Alright, our next
"Morning Show" panel,

00:37:35.033 --> 00:37:37.901
we have Mimi Leder,
Michael Ellenberg,

00:37:37.901 --> 00:37:41.701
Nestor Carbonell
and Brian Stelter.

00:37:41.701 --> 00:37:43.100
These are four
of the smartest people

00:37:43.100 --> 00:37:44.334
that are working in Hollywood.

00:37:44.334 --> 00:37:46.133
I have to say,
it just so happened

00:37:46.133 --> 00:37:49.501
that the four of you
are right exactly.

00:37:49.501 --> 00:37:51.534
He did.

00:37:51.534 --> 00:37:53.734
-Don't let it fool you.
Please don't.

00:37:56.300 --> 00:37:58.834
-Nestor, if he hadn't mentioned
that you had gone to Harvard,

00:37:58.834 --> 00:38:01.801
would you have mentioned it
in your first answer?

00:38:01.801 --> 00:38:03.667
-Well, you know,
if I hadn't, Mimi --

00:38:03.667 --> 00:38:04.701
I'm sorry. Not Mimi.

00:38:04.701 --> 00:38:06.434
But I know Kerry Ehrin
would have.

00:38:06.434 --> 00:38:09.000
She made fun of it
the first episode of our show.

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:11.167
It was about dropping the --
dropping --

00:38:11.167 --> 00:38:12.634
Well, they they called it...

00:38:12.634 --> 00:38:14.167
-"Harvard, Harvard, Harvard."

00:38:14.167 --> 00:38:15.501
-Yeah, "Harvard, Harvard,
Harvard."

00:38:15.501 --> 00:38:16.901
It was called
dropping the "H" bomb.

00:38:16.901 --> 00:38:19.767
I learned quickly never
to bring that up.

00:38:19.767 --> 00:38:21.868
You never bring up
where you go to college

00:38:21.868 --> 00:38:24.400
if you go to that school
because no one wants to hear it.

00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:27.100
-Mimi, I'm gonna start --

00:38:27.100 --> 00:38:28.467
I'm gonna start
this panel with you.

00:38:28.467 --> 00:38:32.334
So you directed four
of the episodes,

00:38:32.334 --> 00:38:33.767
hired all
of the other directors.

00:38:33.767 --> 00:38:37.000
You are the supervising
director.

00:38:37.000 --> 00:38:42.934
This was a show that, you know,
had such gigantic expectations.

00:38:42.934 --> 00:38:44.267
It was Apple.

00:38:44.267 --> 00:38:46.834
It was two of the biggest stars
on the planet.

00:38:46.834 --> 00:38:49.067
It was -- it was about
something

00:38:49.067 --> 00:38:50.734
that everybody
was talking about.

00:38:50.734 --> 00:38:53.801
I know that you must have had
a tremendous amount

00:38:53.801 --> 00:38:56.367
of input from
a lot of different places

00:38:56.367 --> 00:39:00.634
as you were
directing these shows.

00:39:00.634 --> 00:39:05.667
How does a director take all
of that input and then maintain

00:39:05.667 --> 00:39:09.901
a really cohesive vision
for the show, which it has?

00:39:09.901 --> 00:39:11.734
It's one of the things
that's so great about it.

00:39:11.734 --> 00:39:13.000
But also then,

00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:15.667
you know, hand it off
to some other directors

00:39:15.667 --> 00:39:18.601
for a lot of the episodes.

00:39:18.601 --> 00:39:21.534
-Well, you know, I was there,
you know,

00:39:21.534 --> 00:39:23.100
from the very beginning,

00:39:23.100 --> 00:39:26.701
you know, before a script
was even written.

00:39:26.701 --> 00:39:29.467
You know, I was always
interested in this world.

00:39:29.467 --> 00:39:31.501
And I read Brian's book.

00:39:31.501 --> 00:39:35.033
I had dinner
with Michael and I was in.

00:39:35.033 --> 00:39:38.534
And when I developed
the look for the show,

00:39:38.534 --> 00:39:43.167
I wanted it to have
a very strong visual style.

00:39:43.167 --> 00:39:46.801
Obviously,
in the morning-show light,

00:39:46.801 --> 00:39:49.534
on a morning-show set,
it's very bright.

00:39:49.534 --> 00:39:51.934
It's very clean.

00:39:51.934 --> 00:39:54.000
It's kind of perfect.

00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:58.267
But our world is a very
messy world.

00:39:58.267 --> 00:40:00.801
And a lot of, you know,
saturation.

00:40:00.801 --> 00:40:02.501
So I wanted it
to be very colorful.

00:40:02.501 --> 00:40:06.334
I wanted to shoot the women
as you would shoot a man,

00:40:06.334 --> 00:40:07.734
you know, with big close-ups,

00:40:07.734 --> 00:40:09.901
because a lot of DPs,
they don't want to shoot.

00:40:09.901 --> 00:40:14.601
They don't want to go in there
and go tight on women of any age

00:40:14.601 --> 00:40:16.467
because it's like,
"No, no, it's too tight.

00:40:16.467 --> 00:40:17.834
You know, stay back."

00:40:17.834 --> 00:40:24.400
And I really felt that this was
such an exploration of --

00:40:24.400 --> 00:40:25.534
Hate this expression --

00:40:25.534 --> 00:40:28.901
but two very powerful,
complicated women.

00:40:28.901 --> 00:40:33.934
And so I wanted to do big frames
where you see the character,

00:40:33.934 --> 00:40:38.667
character of the city
next to their faces.

00:40:38.667 --> 00:40:44.300
And so you go in and you create
a pilot, you create a look.

00:40:44.300 --> 00:40:48.501
And what you hope for
when you hire other directors

00:40:48.501 --> 00:40:52.234
is that they honor your style.

00:40:52.234 --> 00:40:55.934
And they do, and you hope
that they make it even better.

00:40:55.934 --> 00:40:58.701
You know, because I decided
when we started this show

00:40:58.701 --> 00:41:01.501
that there were gonna be
no visual rules,

00:41:01.501 --> 00:41:04.200
because sometimes you go in
and you make a show and you say,

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:06.601
"Okay, we're not gonna do
any hand-held shots."

00:41:06.601 --> 00:41:11.267
Well, this was a completely
different, you know, approach.

00:41:11.267 --> 00:41:13.133
We could do hand-held.
We could do cranes.

00:41:13.133 --> 00:41:15.767
We could do steady cams.
We could do lock-offs.

00:41:15.767 --> 00:41:17.601
We could do a long lens.

00:41:17.601 --> 00:41:20.734
It was all about
what the scene was about

00:41:20.734 --> 00:41:23.834
and how to best
tell the story visually.

00:41:23.834 --> 00:41:29.968
And, you know, it -- it was
a very detailed look this season

00:41:29.968 --> 00:41:35.200
at how sexual assault happens
to one of our characters

00:41:35.200 --> 00:41:40.200
and, you know, to shoot that,
you had to really get in there

00:41:40.200 --> 00:41:43.234
and get in there emotionally
with the camera.

00:41:43.234 --> 00:41:45.334
And so that's what I tried
to do with the show.

00:41:45.334 --> 00:41:48.167
And I think that's what --
how it was written.

00:41:48.167 --> 00:41:51.968
And, yeah.

00:41:51.968 --> 00:41:53.300
It was a great exploration.

00:41:53.300 --> 00:41:57.200
-Mimi, you know,
Mimi's shepherded

00:41:57.200 --> 00:41:58.634
all of our directors,

00:41:58.634 --> 00:42:05.434
including a dear friend of ours,
Lynn Shelton,

00:42:05.434 --> 00:42:09.334
who did really a wonderful job
for us on the fourth episode.

00:42:09.334 --> 00:42:14.868
And so we're -- we're --
we're heartbroken about her --

00:42:14.868 --> 00:42:16.400
her untimely death.

00:42:16.400 --> 00:42:18.868
-Absolutely. It was shocking.

00:42:18.868 --> 00:42:23.334
And she brought, you know,
her indie cred to the show

00:42:23.334 --> 00:42:26.634
and really put her stamp
on her episode.

00:42:26.634 --> 00:42:29.367
You know, and that's
what really great directors do.

00:42:29.367 --> 00:42:32.300
They come in
and they do something special

00:42:32.300 --> 00:42:34.434
and they make you pay attention

00:42:34.434 --> 00:42:36.334
and you sometimes
don't even know why.

00:42:36.334 --> 00:42:39.234
And that's the best outcome.

00:42:39.234 --> 00:42:42.200
-Michael, one of the things
that is so interesting

00:42:42.200 --> 00:42:45.133
about the show
and really bold of the show

00:42:45.133 --> 00:42:50.234
is that we hear all of these
various male perspectives.

00:42:50.234 --> 00:42:52.834
So, for example,
you know, for example,

00:42:52.834 --> 00:42:55.901
Mitch thinks he hasn't done
anything wrong.

00:42:55.901 --> 00:42:58.200
I think the great line
is, "From the dawn of time,

00:42:58.200 --> 00:43:00.934
men have used their power
to attract women."

00:43:00.934 --> 00:43:05.567
And now he's just angry that,
you know, the rules have changed

00:43:05.567 --> 00:43:07.133
and that
he's being treated unfairly.

00:43:07.133 --> 00:43:11.067
But for the creative
team and the writers,

00:43:11.067 --> 00:43:13.200
was it difficult to,

00:43:13.200 --> 00:43:17.834
you know, get into
the head of a chauvinist

00:43:17.834 --> 00:43:23.434
and then present what
he's thinking and make him human

00:43:23.434 --> 00:43:28.400
without excusing his behavior
or his world view?

00:43:28.400 --> 00:43:30.167
But because you do manage
to do that.

00:43:30.167 --> 00:43:33.534
And I just wonder what
the creative process was like

00:43:33.534 --> 00:43:37.834
in getting to those
to those places in the scripts.

00:43:37.834 --> 00:43:39.200
-Yeah, I mean, obviously,

00:43:39.200 --> 00:43:41.601
Kerry can speak best to it.

00:43:41.601 --> 00:43:47.534
But the show and really her...

00:43:47.534 --> 00:43:51.634
I'd say emotional, thematic
and kind of vision of the world,

00:43:51.634 --> 00:43:54.234
not just the show,
is that the show has

00:43:54.234 --> 00:43:56.934
a very clear moral point
of view,

00:43:56.934 --> 00:43:59.567
but inside that,
her real desire

00:43:59.567 --> 00:44:01.701
was for --
not to have a show where --

00:44:01.701 --> 00:44:03.701
for the audience to feel safe,

00:44:03.701 --> 00:44:07.934
you know, where, like,

00:44:07.934 --> 00:44:09.300
it shouldn't feel like
three years ago,

00:44:09.300 --> 00:44:12.067
everything was fine
and you were good.

00:44:12.067 --> 00:44:14.167
Oh, and here's
someone clearly bad.

00:44:14.167 --> 00:44:18.767
And that didn't feel like a
thing that would have addressed

00:44:18.767 --> 00:44:20.167
the reality of where we were

00:44:20.167 --> 00:44:22.133
and being a kind of
honest reflection

00:44:22.133 --> 00:44:24.968
that would yield real growth
and real depth

00:44:24.968 --> 00:44:26.601
and meaning going forward.

00:44:26.601 --> 00:44:29.200
There was always a desire
to show the humanity

00:44:29.200 --> 00:44:32.033
by people who could do
horrible things on the show

00:44:32.033 --> 00:44:33.634
and people who do good things
in the show

00:44:33.634 --> 00:44:35.934
and have a real reckoning
for all of us watching at home,

00:44:35.934 --> 00:44:38.634
which is, you know, which parts
of all of us, you know,

00:44:38.634 --> 00:44:40.901
we have bits of all these people
in our lives.

00:44:40.901 --> 00:44:44.334
And so it's really delicate.

00:44:44.334 --> 00:44:46.634
You need an amazing writer,
you need amazing actors,

00:44:46.634 --> 00:44:48.133
you need an amazing director

00:44:48.133 --> 00:44:52.234
so that the intention is to get
to something deeper,

00:44:52.234 --> 00:44:54.501
more meaningful without feeling

00:44:54.501 --> 00:44:59.567
like you're trying to excuse
anything.

00:44:59.567 --> 00:45:02.267
And he's also ridiculous,
you know.

00:45:02.267 --> 00:45:04.367
And so there's a humor
in it, too,

00:45:04.367 --> 00:45:07.767
that the show is able
to capture.

00:45:07.767 --> 00:45:09.467
And Carell --
The last thing I'll say

00:45:09.467 --> 00:45:11.901
is I think the casting of Carell
was vital in all of this,

00:45:11.901 --> 00:45:13.267
which is that he was a really --

00:45:13.267 --> 00:45:15.701
is a really brave actor
and a brilliant actor.

00:45:15.701 --> 00:45:17.234
There was a very conscious
intent

00:45:17.234 --> 00:45:18.601
from the beginning,

00:45:18.601 --> 00:45:21.400
which is that to have
someone like Steve,

00:45:21.400 --> 00:45:22.901
who an audience loves,

00:45:22.901 --> 00:45:24.701
admires and thinks of
as their friend

00:45:24.701 --> 00:45:27.400
and as their uncle and someone
they just can't imagine

00:45:27.400 --> 00:45:29.334
would have done
these bad things.

00:45:29.334 --> 00:45:32.167
And so we were trying to very
much echo the experience people

00:45:32.167 --> 00:45:34.701
had watching some
of their most beloved people,

00:45:34.701 --> 00:45:36.434
learning these horrible things
about them.

00:45:36.434 --> 00:45:38.167
And so you could go
on that journey

00:45:38.167 --> 00:45:42.834
and go home with him and see it
and doesn't excuse it,

00:45:42.834 --> 00:45:45.767
but it allows you to be there
and take it in

00:45:45.767 --> 00:45:46.968
and then go on this ride.

00:45:46.968 --> 00:45:49.868
And then when you really see
what happened,

00:45:49.868 --> 00:45:51.701
you're really distressed by it.

00:45:51.701 --> 00:45:53.200
And then that experience,

00:45:53.200 --> 00:45:55.534
it's to earn that episode
with Gugu,

00:45:55.534 --> 00:45:58.167
the flashback episode
in Las Vegas.

00:45:58.167 --> 00:46:02.501
-Nestor, your character, Yanko,
who is the weatherman,

00:46:02.501 --> 00:46:03.667
he is --

00:46:03.667 --> 00:46:06.601
One of the things
that I said earlier this evening

00:46:06.601 --> 00:46:08.434
that I love about the show
is it rushes into

00:46:08.434 --> 00:46:09.634
these gray areas.

00:46:09.634 --> 00:46:12.467
It doesn't just,
you know, not avoid them.

00:46:12.467 --> 00:46:13.868
It rushes into them.

00:46:13.868 --> 00:46:18.000
And, you know,
Yanko is in a relationship

00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:21.934
with a subordinate played
by Bel Powley,

00:46:21.934 --> 00:46:24.367
who plays Claire is her name.

00:46:24.367 --> 00:46:26.033
So when, you know,

00:46:26.033 --> 00:46:28.167
when Yanko and Claire
had their scenes together,

00:46:28.167 --> 00:46:33.634
it was very important that Yanko
not seem like a predator,

00:46:33.634 --> 00:46:36.200
even though he is older
and he's famous

00:46:36.200 --> 00:46:38.167
and he's a big deal on the show.

00:46:38.167 --> 00:46:41.934
How did you and Bel
navigate those scenes together

00:46:41.934 --> 00:46:44.167
and what kind of conversations
did you two have

00:46:44.167 --> 00:46:45.934
about their relationship?

00:46:45.934 --> 00:46:47.968
-Sure.

00:46:47.968 --> 00:46:49.267
That's a great question.

00:46:49.267 --> 00:46:50.434
I mean, it really --

00:46:50.434 --> 00:46:52.467
It's a bit more about
the writing, though,

00:46:52.467 --> 00:46:54.133
in terms of what Kerry
was conceiving

00:46:54.133 --> 00:46:56.734
in terms of this dynamic
and how did it start.

00:46:56.734 --> 00:46:59.400
And then there's a flashback
scene in episode eight,

00:46:59.400 --> 00:47:02.534
I believe, which is sort of
the genesis of this relationship

00:47:02.534 --> 00:47:05.000
where she approaches Yanko --

00:47:05.000 --> 00:47:08.300
Claire approaches Yanko
off the heels

00:47:08.300 --> 00:47:11.400
of watching him do a weathercast
in Puerto Rico.

00:47:11.400 --> 00:47:14.467
And so she's really
initiating the dynamic,

00:47:14.467 --> 00:47:16.467
whereas my character
sort of falls

00:47:16.467 --> 00:47:19.334
into this thing
that she initiates.

00:47:19.334 --> 00:47:22.267
And I think a lot of it
was about intention,

00:47:22.267 --> 00:47:23.467
about motivation, about --

00:47:23.467 --> 00:47:25.167
And so I spoke with Bel
about this,

00:47:25.167 --> 00:47:26.567
like, "How did we meet?"

00:47:26.567 --> 00:47:29.734
You know, we sort of created
a loose history among ourselves,

00:47:29.734 --> 00:47:32.067
like, you know,
even before we shot that scene

00:47:32.067 --> 00:47:35.434
because it was important for it
to be a pure love.

00:47:35.434 --> 00:47:37.033
As you said,
it couldn't be something

00:47:37.033 --> 00:47:39.067
that was sort of lecherous
on his part,

00:47:39.067 --> 00:47:41.133
him being a subordinate.

00:47:41.133 --> 00:47:42.601
And I did --

00:47:42.601 --> 00:47:45.634
You know, the fact that he's
a lot older than her as well,

00:47:45.634 --> 00:47:47.701
obviously plays into it.

00:47:47.701 --> 00:47:50.434
So, yeah, it was important
for us to come up with something

00:47:50.434 --> 00:47:53.167
sort of pure and innocent
and that we had fallen into.

00:47:53.167 --> 00:47:55.868
And then just by the sheer fact
that those things

00:47:55.868 --> 00:48:01.033
are problematic in a workspace,
there's your conflict.

00:48:01.033 --> 00:48:02.300
But -- but we had to play

00:48:02.300 --> 00:48:04.000
against that conflict
as much as we could

00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:07.667
by keeping it as pure
and innocent as possible.

00:48:07.667 --> 00:48:09.534
Yeah.

00:48:09.534 --> 00:48:12.901
-Brian, probably nobody
in the world knows more

00:48:12.901 --> 00:48:14.434
about the news business

00:48:14.434 --> 00:48:17.901
and particularly the TV news
industry than you.

00:48:17.901 --> 00:48:20.801
And what so much of this show
is about

00:48:20.801 --> 00:48:22.701
besides being about power,

00:48:22.701 --> 00:48:25.234
it's about the news media

00:48:25.234 --> 00:48:29.267
and it's about the news'
responsibility

00:48:29.267 --> 00:48:31.767
to hold
the powerful accountable.

00:48:31.767 --> 00:48:37.501
And that is something that has
become more and more difficult

00:48:37.501 --> 00:48:41.200
for the media to do at a time
when the world is so divisive

00:48:41.200 --> 00:48:44.801
and everybody can tune in
to whatever website

00:48:44.801 --> 00:48:48.033
or network will tell them
what they want to hear.

00:48:48.033 --> 00:48:50.934
And, you know, journalists
are being called the enemy,

00:48:50.934 --> 00:48:53.667
you know,
by the leader of our country.

00:48:53.667 --> 00:48:57.734
What do you think
the media needs to do

00:48:57.734 --> 00:49:02.767
in order to try to do its job
and try to do the thing

00:49:02.767 --> 00:49:06.767
that it was supposed to do
against all of these obstacles?

00:49:06.767 --> 00:49:08.868
-First of all, I want to make
you my TV agent,

00:49:08.868 --> 00:49:11.300
because you are very flattering.

00:49:11.300 --> 00:49:12.534
I am grateful.

00:49:12.534 --> 00:49:14.934
Look, everybody
on television, right,

00:49:14.934 --> 00:49:17.434
the Bradleys and Alices
of the world

00:49:17.434 --> 00:49:18.934
of the morning show,

00:49:18.934 --> 00:49:22.701
you see a vulnerability and an
insecurity in those characters.

00:49:22.701 --> 00:49:25.234
I mean, not to mention,
you know,

00:49:25.234 --> 00:49:28.300
this disgraced former anchor
who's been taken down.

00:49:28.300 --> 00:49:31.334
All of them are so insecure,
so vulnerable.

00:49:31.334 --> 00:49:34.734
And yet that is what makes them
so appealing to the viewers,

00:49:34.734 --> 00:49:37.601
so relatable, so powerful.

00:49:37.601 --> 00:49:39.067
And I think
we show power dynamics

00:49:39.067 --> 00:49:41.133
in different ways on this show.

00:49:41.133 --> 00:49:42.434
And we talked about it
on one level,

00:49:42.434 --> 00:49:44.767
the Me Too movement
and those power dynamics,

00:49:44.767 --> 00:49:46.534
but also who has
the power on air?

00:49:46.534 --> 00:49:49.033
Is it the anchors
or the producers?

00:49:49.033 --> 00:49:51.067
Is it the anchors
or the executives?

00:49:51.067 --> 00:49:53.067
Who has the power
to shape narratives

00:49:53.067 --> 00:49:55.167
and tell you
what is true in the world?

00:49:55.167 --> 00:49:58.167
I love how this season
begins with Bradley

00:49:58.167 --> 00:50:02.033
at a protest covering a protest
for a conservative news channel

00:50:02.033 --> 00:50:05.067
and then the debates
that she gets into with people.

00:50:05.067 --> 00:50:06.667
I love early in the season,

00:50:06.667 --> 00:50:08.667
the conversation
among executives

00:50:08.667 --> 00:50:11.834
about what news is in this
entertainment-saturated world,

00:50:11.834 --> 00:50:13.634
because those are
real conversations

00:50:13.634 --> 00:50:15.934
that are actually happening
in newsrooms

00:50:15.934 --> 00:50:17.434
and in media companies.

00:50:17.434 --> 00:50:19.567
And if you think about what's
happened the past three months,

00:50:19.567 --> 00:50:21.100
like, I'm here
in my spare bedroom.

00:50:21.100 --> 00:50:23.334
I got a camera behind me
because this is the studio

00:50:23.334 --> 00:50:26.133
where me and my wife
do live shots for TV,

00:50:26.133 --> 00:50:28.667
like, everything has gone upside
down in the last three months.

00:50:28.667 --> 00:50:30.167
And I think in some ways

00:50:30.167 --> 00:50:32.901
that's helped with transparency
with the viewers.

00:50:32.901 --> 00:50:35.868
I think in real-world media,
real-world news,

00:50:35.868 --> 00:50:37.400
the more honest and transparent

00:50:37.400 --> 00:50:39.767
and relatable we can be,
the better.

00:50:39.767 --> 00:50:43.501
And morning shows do that more
effectively than anything else.

00:50:43.501 --> 00:50:45.434
You know, when you're watching
Savannah Guthrie

00:50:45.434 --> 00:50:48.000
or Robin Roberts or Gayle King,

00:50:48.000 --> 00:50:51.968
you know, you're inside their
world like no other time of day,

00:50:51.968 --> 00:50:53.634
like no other form of media.

00:50:53.634 --> 00:50:55.701
I think that's why this show
is so relatable

00:50:55.701 --> 00:50:57.334
and so compelling
for people as well,

00:50:57.334 --> 00:51:01.501
because everybody wants to know
what it was really like

00:51:01.501 --> 00:51:03.834
for a male anchor
who was fired.

00:51:03.834 --> 00:51:04.901
They want to know
what it was like

00:51:04.901 --> 00:51:07.033
for that person
at home afterwards.

00:51:07.033 --> 00:51:09.801
They want to know what it was
like for the staff afterwards.

00:51:09.801 --> 00:51:11.634
And, you know, Kerry and Mimi
and Mike,

00:51:11.634 --> 00:51:13.834
everyone's dramatized this

00:51:13.834 --> 00:51:16.868
and let people see
behind the curtain

00:51:16.868 --> 00:51:18.968
in a way that
no other show ever has.

00:51:18.968 --> 00:51:25.467
-Mimi, was there a --
was there a particular sequence

00:51:25.467 --> 00:51:30.267
or scene that when you think
back on the first season,

00:51:30.267 --> 00:51:34.968
was particularly
challenging to get

00:51:34.968 --> 00:51:36.067
exactly right?

00:51:36.067 --> 00:51:37.968
There were, you know,
there are so many

00:51:37.968 --> 00:51:40.300
difficult conversations
and characters.

00:51:40.300 --> 00:51:45.934
There are so many interactions
between people.

00:51:45.934 --> 00:51:49.334
Gosh, I mean, I could, you know,
the scenes between

00:51:49.334 --> 00:51:52.000
Bradley and Hannah alone,

00:51:52.000 --> 00:51:56.334
but tell me one that was
particularly tricky to get right

00:51:56.334 --> 00:51:58.567
and how you did it.

00:51:58.567 --> 00:52:04.100
-Well, there were so many
that were challenging.

00:52:04.100 --> 00:52:07.868
I would say the scene
where --

00:52:07.868 --> 00:52:09.100
I would say the scene

00:52:09.100 --> 00:52:12.100
where Bradley
is at the protest

00:52:12.100 --> 00:52:14.067
was an extremely
challenging scene.

00:52:14.067 --> 00:52:15.267
It was Reese's --

00:52:15.267 --> 00:52:18.300
Actually, I think
her first scene that she shot.

00:52:18.300 --> 00:52:21.167
So there we were
in the middle,

00:52:21.167 --> 00:52:23.968
you know,
in this kind of dirty mine.

00:52:23.968 --> 00:52:25.934
You know, it's
100-degree weather

00:52:25.934 --> 00:52:30.067
trying to figure out
the levels of anger,

00:52:30.067 --> 00:52:33.701
the levels of emotion,
the measure and the level

00:52:33.701 --> 00:52:37.534
of how much this character
loses her s---.

00:52:37.534 --> 00:52:41.434
How far does
this character go?

00:52:41.434 --> 00:52:45.067
And, you know,
when an actor first hits,

00:52:45.067 --> 00:52:47.868
you know,
puts their feet in their shoes,

00:52:47.868 --> 00:52:50.400
they're just, you know,
they're just touching,

00:52:50.400 --> 00:52:53.801
you know, just trying to figure
who they are.

00:52:53.801 --> 00:52:56.734
And so, it was a big challenge
to throw her in

00:52:56.734 --> 00:53:01.234
and have her scream
at this guy and call him out.

00:53:01.234 --> 00:53:04.000
And it was, you know,
one of her Achilles heels

00:53:04.000 --> 00:53:07.067
in that she -- this was the
thing that has held her back

00:53:07.067 --> 00:53:09.434
in her career
is this character.

00:53:09.434 --> 00:53:11.467
And so it was really hard to,

00:53:11.467 --> 00:53:13.868
in the middle of 300 extras,

00:53:13.868 --> 00:53:16.667
to modulate that performance.

00:53:16.667 --> 00:53:19.067
But, you know,
she's a brilliant actress.

00:53:19.067 --> 00:53:21.701
And we worked
really hard on it.

00:53:21.701 --> 00:53:22.901
And we found it.

00:53:22.901 --> 00:53:24.901
And it was really challenging.

00:53:24.901 --> 00:53:28.767
And Jen Aniston's first scene
that she did

00:53:28.767 --> 00:53:33.801
in the season
was her last scene in the pilot,

00:53:33.801 --> 00:53:37.868
where she goes to Mitch's house.

00:53:37.868 --> 00:53:40.267
And she also had to go,
you know,

00:53:40.267 --> 00:53:44.133
90, 100 miles an hour

00:53:44.133 --> 00:53:48.200
and also find those grace notes
and also find the quiet

00:53:48.200 --> 00:53:51.000
and all the stuff
between the lines.

00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:54.000
And so, you know, the material
is very challenging.

00:53:54.000 --> 00:53:55.501
It's brilliantly written.

00:53:55.501 --> 00:53:57.868
You know, it's such a gift,

00:53:57.868 --> 00:54:02.801
this show that, you know,
Brian gave us

00:54:02.801 --> 00:54:04.834
and this world

00:54:04.834 --> 00:54:07.167
that we are peeling
all these layers of.

00:54:07.167 --> 00:54:10.534
And, you know, we have Nestor.

00:54:10.534 --> 00:54:13.033
We have all these
brilliant actors,

00:54:13.033 --> 00:54:17.734
Jen and Reese and Desean
and Mark Duplass, Billy Crudup.

00:54:17.734 --> 00:54:19.434
I know I'm leaving someone out.

00:54:19.434 --> 00:54:22.367
You know, Karen Pittman,
Gugu Mbatha-Raw.

00:54:22.367 --> 00:54:25.501
And it's -- it's just --
it's a joy

00:54:25.501 --> 00:54:27.367
to come to work and say,

00:54:27.367 --> 00:54:29.334
"Okay, what are we gonna
figure out today?

00:54:29.334 --> 00:54:33.200
How are we gonna get
to the center of this scene?

00:54:33.200 --> 00:54:35.100
What is the scene about?"

00:54:35.100 --> 00:54:39.234
And it's just been
really challenging

00:54:39.234 --> 00:54:40.968
and really fun, you know,

00:54:40.968 --> 00:54:44.667
finding these details
and telling this...

00:54:44.667 --> 00:54:47.400
you know, showing this world
and the messiness

00:54:47.400 --> 00:54:52.234
and how complicated it is
and how f------- up we all are.

00:54:52.234 --> 00:54:54.334
-And if I can just add to that,
when I wrote my book

00:54:54.334 --> 00:54:56.801
about morning TV many years ago,

00:54:56.801 --> 00:54:59.300
I came away loving
these shows even more.

00:54:59.300 --> 00:55:00.501
Like, yes, there was drama.

00:55:00.501 --> 00:55:02.734
Yes, there were scandals
and intrigue,

00:55:02.734 --> 00:55:04.534
but I came away
loving the shows

00:55:04.534 --> 00:55:06.667
and appreciating
the shows even more.

00:55:06.667 --> 00:55:09.200
And when you brought up just,
you know,

00:55:09.200 --> 00:55:12.534
enemy of the people rhetoric,
outrageous and hateful things

00:55:12.534 --> 00:55:15.167
said by the President and other
politicians in this environment

00:55:15.167 --> 00:55:17.300
we're in
in the real world,

00:55:17.300 --> 00:55:20.667
one way to combat those lies
and smears is by showing

00:55:20.667 --> 00:55:23.534
who we are, showing the people,
showing the real people.

00:55:23.534 --> 00:55:24.901
And I think what
this show does,

00:55:24.901 --> 00:55:28.000
it dramatizes what it's like
for real journalists,

00:55:28.000 --> 00:55:29.601
what the real pressures are,

00:55:29.601 --> 00:55:31.400
what the real tensions
are between,

00:55:31.400 --> 00:55:33.901
you know, straight vanilla
news and entertainment,

00:55:33.901 --> 00:55:37.367
like, that kind of conversation,
that kind of attention

00:55:37.367 --> 00:55:40.467
is what disproves enemy
of the people lies.

00:55:40.467 --> 00:55:44.234
That kind of demonstration
of how newsrooms really work

00:55:44.234 --> 00:55:48.501
is what counters those smears
and bulls---.

00:55:49.968 --> 00:55:52.400
-But what Mimi has neglected
to say,

00:55:52.400 --> 00:55:55.434
which is instrumental
in executing this,

00:55:55.434 --> 00:55:59.033
is creating an environment,
so that someone like, you know,

00:55:59.033 --> 00:56:00.834
when you have a scene
like where Bradley,

00:56:00.834 --> 00:56:03.467
you know, Reese's first scene
or Jen's first scene

00:56:03.467 --> 00:56:06.067
seems insurmountable for anyone
to tackle that scene,

00:56:06.067 --> 00:56:08.400
let alone for that
to be their first scene.

00:56:08.400 --> 00:56:10.834
Is that you have someone
set the stage, you know,

00:56:10.834 --> 00:56:14.267
like Mimi, to have
a relaxed environment.

00:56:14.267 --> 00:56:15.667
And that is just --

00:56:15.667 --> 00:56:18.367
it's very hard
to do for any director.

00:56:18.367 --> 00:56:19.868
And I remember talking
to Bel about this.

00:56:19.868 --> 00:56:21.000
We had a tough scene.

00:56:21.000 --> 00:56:22.767
One of our first scenes
was tough.

00:56:22.767 --> 00:56:25.200
And it was like, I felt
every time I talked to you,

00:56:25.200 --> 00:56:26.934
Mimi, it was almost like
I felt like

00:56:26.934 --> 00:56:28.968
there was also a therapist
there, you know,

00:56:28.968 --> 00:56:30.934
and you do.

00:56:30.934 --> 00:56:32.734
You create
this beautiful environment

00:56:32.734 --> 00:56:36.367
that allows for actors
to let go

00:56:36.367 --> 00:56:38.801
and trust, you know,
and just be there.

00:56:38.801 --> 00:56:41.234
And that is the truth.
I mean, that's what --

00:56:41.234 --> 00:56:44.167
I mean, among many other things
that you do beautifully,

00:56:44.167 --> 00:56:46.501
that's just instrumental
into any scene

00:56:46.501 --> 00:56:48.567
is to have
everyone's guard down

00:56:48.567 --> 00:56:50.133
and just to be raw and honest.

00:56:50.133 --> 00:56:52.667
And certainly this show
called for it.

00:56:52.667 --> 00:56:54.400
Whenever you work
with Kerry Ehrin,

00:56:54.400 --> 00:56:56.667
you're gonna be called
to be as raw as you can be

00:56:56.667 --> 00:56:58.300
and you set the stage for that.

00:56:58.300 --> 00:57:00.434
And that's -- that was huge.

00:57:00.434 --> 00:57:02.200
To have you there
at the helm is enormous.

00:57:02.200 --> 00:57:03.400
-Thank you.

00:57:03.400 --> 00:57:05.834
-What is happening right now
with the show?

00:57:05.834 --> 00:57:08.267
Is everybody
in the writers' room

00:57:08.267 --> 00:57:10.033
but working remotely?

00:57:10.033 --> 00:57:11.133
Where are we?

00:57:11.133 --> 00:57:13.501
-Yes, the writers
are working remotely.

00:57:13.501 --> 00:57:17.234
And, you know,
it's a new -- the new normal.

00:57:17.234 --> 00:57:19.834
And we are, you know,
we were in the middle

00:57:19.834 --> 00:57:25.067
of shooting the first episode
when we shut down.

00:57:25.067 --> 00:57:30.501
And, you know, we are working
all the COVID protocols

00:57:30.501 --> 00:57:34.334
and figuring out
how to go back to work safely.

00:57:34.334 --> 00:57:37.767
And that's what we're in
the middle of doing

00:57:37.767 --> 00:57:40.901
and fine-tuning our scripts.

00:57:40.901 --> 00:57:43.133
-Is everybody shooting
for, like, early --

00:57:43.133 --> 00:57:45.400
I know the, you know,
probably ideally

00:57:45.400 --> 00:57:48.033
the second season
would have started in November.

00:57:48.033 --> 00:57:52.234
Are you thinking now
maybe early 2021?

00:57:52.234 --> 00:57:54.167
-You mean when you see it?

00:57:54.167 --> 00:57:55.667
-When I will see it.

00:57:55.667 --> 00:57:56.834
Oh, yeah. When I'll see it.

00:57:56.834 --> 00:57:58.534
When you'll be done
and it'll air.

00:57:58.534 --> 00:58:01.534
-Yeah. I mean -- I mean --
Yeah. Yeah.

00:58:01.534 --> 00:58:05.267
I don't think we'll be able
to finish shooting this year.

00:58:05.267 --> 00:58:08.033
But the, you know,
I've talked to Apple, obviously,

00:58:08.033 --> 00:58:10.467
about when will it air
and be back?

00:58:10.467 --> 00:58:11.701
-Alright.

00:58:11.701 --> 00:58:13.868
-There's gonna be a lot
of shows like that.

00:58:13.868 --> 00:58:15.634
-Yeah.

00:58:15.634 --> 00:58:18.701
-I can't wait.
Thank you, all of you.

00:58:18.701 --> 00:58:20.868
Nestor Carbonell, Mimi Leder,
Michael Ellenberg,

00:58:20.868 --> 00:58:22.200
Brian Stelter.

00:58:22.200 --> 00:58:23.567
And thank you.

00:58:23.567 --> 00:58:25.501
We're out of time,

00:58:25.501 --> 00:58:27.734
but thank you
for everybody who joined in.

00:58:27.734 --> 00:58:29.801
And listen, thanks to the actors
and producers

00:58:29.801 --> 00:58:32.834
for taking time to come on
and answer our questions.

00:58:32.834 --> 00:58:35.801
And thank you, Apple TV+
for making it all happen

00:58:35.801 --> 00:58:37.267
and including me in it.

00:58:37.267 --> 00:58:40.834
Everybody stay safe
and stay healthy. Bye.

00:58:40.834 --> 00:58:42.000
-Thank you.
-Thank you, Jess.

00:58:42.000 --> 00:58:43.434
-This is...

00:58:43.434 --> 00:58:45.367
-Dramatic.

00:58:45.367 --> 00:58:48.367
Thrilling.

00:58:48.367 --> 00:58:50.801
-You stole my life with this.

00:58:50.801 --> 00:58:53.567
You left me in the woods
with a pack of wolves

00:58:53.567 --> 00:58:56.200
-This is an important story.

00:58:56.200 --> 00:58:58.234
-What if that takes
down the network,

00:58:58.234 --> 00:58:59.767
the show, and everybody on it?

00:58:59.767 --> 00:59:01.467
-Is that a risk worth taking?

00:59:05.400 --> 00:59:07.634
-Get on this ride.
It's gonna be a good one.

